{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>He shall not defile himself, a husband among his people, to profane himself.</b> The reason a priest may not defile himself except for his close relatives is that the priest is indeed a master among his people for understanding and instruction, for <b>שִׂפְתֵי כֹהֵן יִשְׁמְרוּ דַעַת וְתוֹרָה יְבַקְשׁוּ מִפִּיהוּ</b> (Malachi 2:7), and it is fitting for such a man to conduct himself with dignity so that his words may be heeded. It is not fitting that he profane his consecration to the sanctuary and its holy things in order to honor the dead who are not his close relatives, as the Sages of blessed memory said (Sanhedrin 47) — that burial and eulogy for the dead constitute the honor due to those who have passed. However, for his close relatives He permitted defilement, for their honor is his own honor."], :book :leviticus, :voice :sforno, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T16:37:10.616255967Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}